WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:40.000
[Music]

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.520
[Narrator:] As the use of radioactive materials
increases,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.660
the problems associated with the disposal

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.380
of the wastes become ever more important.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.100
Of all known toxic materials,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.719
radioactive nuclides alone cannot be detoxified

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:03.319
or rendered harmless by any known chemical
or physical processing.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.460
Safe disposal of these materials requires
that they be so segregated

00:01:08.469 --> 00:01:11.930
that any return to man is in such a low concentration

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.260
as to be certainly harmless.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.920
All manner of possible return paths must be
considered

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:26.860
in choosing a processing and disposal method.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.310
Disposal by burial on land requires the utmost
care

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.259
to ensure that there is a negligible contact
between

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:40.530
the waste product and water sources

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.130
through which the isotopes might return to
man,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.570
either directly or through food chains.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:52.340
When intermediate products such as edible
plants, animals,

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:54.030
and fish are involved,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.250
the effects of biological concentrations of
particular nuclides

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:02.009
in these intermediates must be carefully appraised.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.390
The most common radioactive waste problems
concern the relatively

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.180
small user of isotopes obtained primarily
from nuclear reactors.

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:19.710
The high level waste originating from reactor
operation itself,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.860
and the associated chemical processing,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.820
require special handling at the installations
where the wastes originate.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.920
The National Institutes of Health is an organization

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.340
devoted to research in clinical medicine

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.489
and the associated basic sciences.

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:44.640
Some 500 members of the scientific staff use
about 100 curies

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.870
of radioactive isotopes each year.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.260
Because of the biological emphasis of the
research program,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.730
carbon-14 and hydrogen-3, or tritium,

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.090
are the most commonly used nuclides.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.920
Waste disposal methods must take into account

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.980
the particular properties of these isotopes.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.870
At the National Institutes of Health,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.209
the Radiation Safety Office is the organization

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:12.840
responsible for all radiation safety operations.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.840
These operations include the processing

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.150
and disposal of radioactive wastes.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.819
Safety office functions center around the
isotope laboratory,

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:26.259
located apart from other laboratories and
service buildings.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.400
The disposal methods to be shown are the result

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.129
of over ten years of development

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:37.209
in cooperation with the United States Coast
Guard.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:43.169
The deep sea was chosen as the most desirable
ultimate depository.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.019
Burial at sea must be carried out so there
will be no interference

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:51.540
with either commercial or sport fishing.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.370
No significant concentrations of any isotope
deposited must appear

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.849
at the surface of the sea or on any beach.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:03.319
The waste disposal area, approximately 90
miles due east of Norfolk,

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:05.870
is over 1,000 fathoms in depth.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.560
Radioactive waste processing and packaging
is carried out

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.370
in a restricted area near the isotope laboratory.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:19.220
This area is surrounded by a custodial fence
to separate it

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.100
from the activities and traffic of the Institutes.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:28.600
Radioactive wastes must be classified into
several different types,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.100
and the disposal governed accordingly.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:45.920
Here a chemical experiment is originating
wastes of varying levels of activity.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.960
Samples are being pipetted out for measuring preparatory to initiating the proper disposal procedures.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.800
These liquid wastes will be assorted

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.300
depending upon the level of activity

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.340
as measured with a portable survey meter.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.940
Carefully measured samples are placed in the
counting

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:21.210
planchets to obtain the standardized volume

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.970
for checking with the scintillation counter.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.210
All of these manipulations take place inside
a hood

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.550
with absorbing paper underneath materials

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.510
so that spills can be readily discarded.

00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:42.310
The waste of low activity will be disposed
of directly

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:44.009
into the regular sewer system,

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:50.909
with water flushing the sample down the drain
at the disposal time.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.259
This other waste, which was found to contain
somewhat greater activity,

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:06.910
will be put into a special sewer system.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:09.569
This leads to a large underground retention
tank

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.259
where the waste will be held for an appreciable
length of time

00:06:12.259 --> 00:06:15.960
before being released to the municipal sewer
system.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.770
Both of these wastes are short half-life,

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:26.070
or else disposal by either of these means
would not be allowable.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.940
Waste containing carbon-14 or tritium cannot
be classified

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:43.350
by the crude assay method previously shown.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:48.130
With these weak beta emitters, carefully prepared
samples are counted in a liquid scintillator.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.199
The scintillating solution,

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:53.770
and the photo multiplier tubes picking up
the tiny flashes of light,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.539
are kept at a very low temperature

00:06:55.539 --> 00:06:59.100
in a deep freeze to reduce the effect of extraneous
disturbances

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.380
on the electric circuits.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:04.030
Carbon-14 and tritium wastes that are too
concentrated

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.039
for direct disposal will be kept for burial
at sea.

00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:10.789
Each of these nuclides have a half-life that
is too long

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:15.429
for appreciable decay to occur during any
practical storage period.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.300
Patient treatment areas generate large quantities

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.259
of contaminated articles, with bed linens
an outstanding example.

00:07:26.259 --> 00:07:28.930
Wherever possible, patients receiving radioisotopes

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:32.150
handle their own urine collections in the
treatment areas.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:34.470
Urines containing gamma emitters are kept

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:39.050
in heavily shielded containers and are collected
at frequent intervals.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.080
Another source of active waste material

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.139
comes from animal experiments.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:47.560
This animal has been injected

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.250
with a long-lived radioactive material.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.039
Note that the experimenters are wearing rubber
gloves

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:55.559
to avoid contamination of hands.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.320
Blood samples are withdrawn,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.490
placed into counting planchets

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:07.169
for drying, and subsequent assay.

00:08:07.169 --> 00:08:08.020
Marking tape is used

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.310
whenever radioactive material is involved.

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:14.050
It is most important that all packages be
so marked,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.330
and an approximate value given of the amount

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:21.310
of the isotope present, and the isotope identified.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.961
Animal carcasses are stored at low temperature

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.860
until ready for disposal by approved methods.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.370
Since licenses for radioactive materials are
given

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.169
by possession limits,

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:42.620
it is necessary to keep a careful account
of the isotopes that are thrown away.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.780
Each package must be marked with the amount
of the isotope which it contains.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.180
Throughout all waste disposal operations and
processing,

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.700
accurate records are kept of radiation exposure

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.240
of all personnel involved.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.180
Film badge monitoring devices are loaded

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.300
and prepared for issue throughout the institutes.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:17.790
A most important part of the monitoring consists
of keeping records.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.640
Most of these records will be needed perhaps

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.440
five to ten years after they have been entered,

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.890
so neatness, accuracy and legibility

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:28.220
are most important items in the record keeping.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:30.930
Records must be kept of all radioactive waste
materials

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.230
that are disposed of by whatever means.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:40.260
This represents about one-half of a day's
collection

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.120
from the laboratories and clinical areas at
the Institutes.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.440
Delivery of radioactive materials to the radiation
safety office

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.860
is made so easy that there will be little
or no temptation

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.040
to dispose of these materials by other methods.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:00.120
Otherwise, these radioactive materials might
be found in the regular trash containers,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.880
or dumped indiscriminately into the municipal
sewer system.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.850
The polyethylene bags are the liners of the waste containers

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:20.210
which are handed out to all users of active
materials.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.360
Wastes are classified and records kept of
each package,

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:28.120
its location, and the method of final disposal.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.680
Here, materials of short half-life are being
kept for decay

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.060
and for later incineration.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.361
Materials which have too long a half-life
to allow decay to take place

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:45.260
will be put into children-sized concrete burial
vaults

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.420
for ultimate disposal at sea.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.660
Liquids, solids, and activities of many different
levels

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:57.510
are some of the diverse materials which are
collected.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:04.220
Vaults are standard commercially available
containers of convenient size.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:09.589
They are made of steel-reinforced concrete
and have lifting eyes cast into the bottom.

00:11:09.589 --> 00:11:13.689
Short lengths of steel cable are attached
to these eyes for handling.

00:11:17.339 --> 00:11:19.300
The vaults are packed as carefully as possible

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.279
to include a maximum amount of material.

00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:24.619
This cuts down the number of containers required,

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:28.480
and aids in obtaining the density required to ensure
sinking in the ocean.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:37.760
Special waste products require special methods
of handling.

00:11:37.769 --> 00:11:42.490
Here are the steps that lead to the ultimate
disposal of some cobalt pellets.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:47.200
They have already been packaged in lead and
welded into a steel pipe.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.860
Spacing rings encircle the cobalt container
so that the pipe

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:52.950
will be centered into the steel disposal drum.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.380
The drum will then be filled with concrete.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.860
Although there is some gamma ray activity

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.070
at the surface of the steel container,

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:05.390
the handling is done without intervening shields
because of the relatively short time involved.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.900
Observe how the centering devices help as
the pipe is lowered into the drum.

00:12:17.029 --> 00:12:21.889
Note the marking of the drum to indicate that
it now contains radioactive materials,

00:12:21.889 --> 00:12:24.769
the amount and isotope being marked on the
label.

00:12:29.589 --> 00:12:31.589
A wet concrete mortar is now poured

00:12:31.589 --> 00:12:34.930
around the steel container containing the
cobalt pellets.

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:37.730
The concrete in this particular case fills
the drum readily

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:38.740
without any tamping,

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:42.460
and a solid mass will result without any agitation.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.240
The concrete can be mixed to any desired consistency.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:55.940
The steel pipe is suspended from the top of
the drum

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:01.890
to prevent it sinking through the soft mass
before the concrete hardens.

00:13:11.139 --> 00:13:15.170
An immediate check is made of the surface
gamma activity of the container.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:16.570
This is to make sure that it will comply

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.980
with all of the interstate commerce regulations

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:29.460
governing the shipment of radioactive materials
over state highways.

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:43.000
The markings on the drums are made in accordance
with requirements of AEC licensing.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.709
They cover the amount and the isotope contained
therein,

00:13:46.709 --> 00:13:50.760
also identifying marks, so that if there is
any future recovery,

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:56.160
the origin of the drum can be identified.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.720
Waste materials are packed in the concrete
burial vaults

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.149
and mixed intimately with a thin concrete
mortar.

00:14:01.149 --> 00:14:03.970
All of the polyethylene containers are broken

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.639
to ensure an intimate mix with the concrete

00:14:06.639 --> 00:14:08.649
and also to obtain a maximum density

00:14:08.649 --> 00:14:10.850
to ensure sinking in the ocean.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:12.750
The vaults are shallow enough to permit

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.890
good mixing of the contents with thin concrete.

00:14:22.639 --> 00:14:24.730
The safety office personnel working directly

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:27.649
over the vaults wear respirators to prevent
the inhalation

00:14:27.649 --> 00:14:30.339
of airborne radioactive materials.

00:14:30.339 --> 00:14:33.110
This is not considered necessary for the cement
workers

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:36.870
who have a casual contact and are not working
directly over the vault

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:40.459
during the active breaking of the polyethylene
containers.

00:14:40.459 --> 00:14:41.970
The vaults are actively agitated

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:45.240
during the entire filling operation to eliminate
voids

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:48.269
and obtain as intimate mixing as possible.

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:51.970
Actual tests show that the finished containers
remain intact

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:56.430
under the hydrostatic pressures at depths
of at least 6,000 feet.

00:15:03.329 --> 00:15:06.560
Vacuum pumps used on a radon plant are contaminated

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:10.970
to an extent where decontamination would be
more expensive than replacement,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.470
so they are disposed of along with other types
of waste.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.240
This is a sample of the wastes collected at
an institution

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.180
such as the National Institutes of Health.

00:15:29.589 --> 00:15:32.839
The vaults are finally sealed with a few inches of richer cement mix

00:15:32.839 --> 00:15:35.060
than is used in the body of the filling.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.899
These vaults shown here represent about one
day's processing

00:15:38.899 --> 00:15:43.630
and contain approximately six months' waste
supply from the Institutes.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.269
Upon completion they are checked for gross
gamma activity

00:15:46.269 --> 00:15:49.869
with a gamma-sensitive meter.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:03.000
Each vault must be marked with identifying
symbols

00:16:03.009 --> 00:16:07.290
to show the isotope contained therein, the
approximate amount,

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:10.320
the authorization permits under which shipment
is made,

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:15.170
and marks and dates for NIH identification
purposes.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.630
The radioactive materials contained in these
vaults

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:20.350
are considered to be class D poisons.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:21.730
And therefore require the use of

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:23.990
Interstate Commerce Commission labels,

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:28.070
as well as direct identification as radioactive
materials.

00:16:30.529 --> 00:16:33.709
The new concrete surfaces are painted to reduce
moisture absorption

00:16:33.709 --> 00:16:35.190
while awaiting final disposition.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:45.240
The  finished vaults are now checked to be sure there is no surface contamination.

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:47.120
This is done by making a series of wipes

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:50.540
across the surface of the finished concrete.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.820
These wipes are then counted in a gas-flow
counter.

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:06.050
This is required because of the possible presence

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:10.890
of either carbon-14 or tritium, both of which
are beta emitters.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.720
An automatic counter is used, each wipe being
counted

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.860
for a preset number of counts,

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:26.140
with the time required being recorded on a
paper tape.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.380
If the counts are within prescribed limits,
essentially zero,

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:40.059
the vaults can then be handled without gloves
from here on.

00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:42.490
If positive contamination is found on the
surface,

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.380
it will be removed by scrubbing

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.950
or by covering the contaminant with a heavy
coat of paint

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.470
or some other covering material.

00:17:56.740 --> 00:18:00.080
Upon completion of the counts, the counting
rate is carefully noted

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:03.440
and becomes a part of the record of each finished
container.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.000
As the vaults are made ready for loading into
trucks

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.200
for transportation to the port of embarkation,

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:17.000
a final gamma check is made on each individual
vault

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:21.370
to be sure that it fulfills the requirements
for transportation over public roads.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.940
Each truck is loaded so that very low activity vaults,

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:31.280
or those containing only nonpenetrating beta emitters,

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:33.500
are placed next to the driver.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.419
This provides additional shielding between
him and any vaults

00:18:36.419 --> 00:18:40.000
which show an appreciable gamma activity at
the surface.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.940
This precaution is perhaps unnecessary but is carried out simply because

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:48.120
because any radioactive exposure should be avoided whenever possible.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:54.840
Each convoy is accompanied by a trained monitor
in a separate vehicle

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:58.370
carrying monitoring instruments capable of
making measurements

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:02.120
at any radioactive emergency which may arise
during the shipment.

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:05.380
A truck convoy leaving for the port of embarkation

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.880
must comply with a variety of regulations.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:17.560
Weight and vehicle spacings while underway
are prescribed by state law.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:20.919
The trucks, pulling into an official state
weighing station,

00:19:20.919 --> 00:19:25.140
have red lights flashing, merely to indicate
a turn off the main highway.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.600
The lights are not a part of the procedures
associated

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.960
with the shipment of the radioactive materials.

00:19:33.169 --> 00:19:38.049
Truck loadings are made with due regard for
the loads allowed by state regulations.

00:19:38.049 --> 00:19:41.970
Each finished vault weighs about 1,500 pounds
in air.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:46.160
Each shipment will have a total weight of
20 to 30 tons.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:48.809
After weights are checked the convoy proceeds,

00:19:48.809 --> 00:19:52.249
at all times following the regulations required
for convoys.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.480
Proper spacing between vehicles is maintained

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:02.110
so that overtaking vehicles need not pass
the convoy as a unit,

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:04.700
but can pull in between the trucks if necessary.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:08.850
In general, the convoying sedan follows behind
the last truck

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:12.650
to take care of any stragglers who may have
mechanical difficulties.

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.909
The convoy ends at the Coast Guard base in Norfolk, Virginia

00:20:19.289 --> 00:20:20.950
where the waste containers are loaded

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.710
onto the fantail of the Coast Guard cutter.

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.980
This vessel is equipped with steel trays which
prevent

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:30.140
contact of the waste container with the wooden
deck,

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:32.480
and in addition raise the shipment to a level

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.140
that will clear the rail of the ship

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.600
during the dumping procedure.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.830
One of the larger trays shown here

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.690
is equipped with sections of roller conveyors
to facilitate

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.809
the off-loading of the concrete vaults

00:20:43.809 --> 00:20:46.200
at the designated dumping site.

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.100
The steel platform is for handling the steel
drums.

00:20:54.409 --> 00:20:56.750
Each set of concrete vaults is locked in position

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.700
by a special locking device which can be tripped
at sea

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.620
for the safe release of the containers.

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:09.460
As in the case of loading the trucks,

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:10.830
preference is given to those vaults

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.240
showing some gamma activity at the surface

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:15.820
by placing them outboard and away from the
positions

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:17.960
which will be occupied by the crew

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.960
during the normal working of the vessel.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:25.110
It will be noted that here the crew members

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:28.179
are handling the vaults without protective
gloves,

00:21:28.179 --> 00:21:30.390
because of the previous checks which have
shown the surfaces

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:33.530
to be completely free of radioactive contamination.

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:43.880
Before the trucks are released to return to
the Institutes,

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:46.950
a final check is made with a thin window counter,

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.550
to be sure that no contamination has been left in the truck bodies.

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:54.169
When this has been so demonstrated, the signs
are removed,

00:21:54.169 --> 00:21:57.649
and the trucks are available for regular duties
without restrictions.

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:06.840
Aboard ship, steel drums are lashed down to
prevent movement

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.380
during the voyage out to the dumping ground.

00:22:13.059 --> 00:22:15.220
The hands and feet of the crew members are
checked

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:17.650
to be sure that no contamination has been
received

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:21.059
by any of them in loading the vaults and drums.

00:22:21.059 --> 00:22:23.000
These surveys are conducted with a Geiger
counter

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:27.049
which will respond to carbon-14 and more energetic emitters,

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:29.700
but which will not detect tritium.

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:31.240
With a thorough mixing of the wastes,

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:34.320
it is hard to imagine a contamination of tritium alone.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:43.320
And so an absence of activity is assumed to
indicate an absence of tritium also.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.540
Tripping a dog releases four vaults

00:22:56.549 --> 00:22:58.960
which go overside almost simultaneously

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.530
but without any damaging collisions.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:02.929
This is done at the dumping ground

00:23:02.929 --> 00:23:05.250
prescribed in the waste disposal license

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:10.190
granted by the Atomic Energy Commission.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:24.900
Experiments conducted at depths of 1,000 fathoms
or 6,000 feet

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.570
suggest that about 15 to 20 minutes is required

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:30.409
for containers to reach the bottom.

00:23:30.409 --> 00:23:34.850
All materials are disposed of at a depth of
not less than 1,000 fathoms,

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:39.690
which in the Norfolk area means approximately
80 to 90 miles due east,

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.780
to carry the ship beyond the continental shelf.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:51.830
Here the fathometer reading for one particular trip indicates

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:56.409
that disposal is made at a depth of 1,000
fathoms.

00:23:56.409 --> 00:24:12.169
[Music]

